and I'm using that radial gradient

224
00:14:57.549 --> 00:14:59.549
and putting it into my base color.

225
00:14:59.549 --> 00:15:01.549
One thing I want to point out, though, in here,

226
00:15:01.549 --> 00:15:03.549
if I type in DOF,

227
00:15:03.549 --> 00:15:06.549
you'll notice that I have before depth of field,

228
00:15:06.549 --> 00:15:09.970
and that's because my material overall

229
00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:12.289
is a translucent object.

230
00:15:12.289 --> 00:15:14.289
So you want to make sure, it being translucent,

231
00:15:14.289 --> 00:15:16.289
that you don't run into any sorting issues,

232
00:15:16.289 --> 00:15:18.289
and you may want to turn this on.

233
00:15:18.289 --> 00:15:20.289
This really just depends on where things are placed

234
00:15:20.289 --> 00:15:22.289
in your scene, but this can be very helpful.

235
00:15:22.289 --> 00:15:25.320
That's pretty much it for that particular material,

236
00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.320
and it actually works out pretty well.

237
00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:30.320
Get a nice little creepy fog rolling across here.

238
00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:34.320
It's not a high-resolution texture,

239
00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.320
and it almost gives you an optical illusion

240
00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:38.320
if we go up, that's just a card,

241
00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.320
but when you look on it straight,

242
00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.320
it almost looks like there's fog in the distance

243
00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:43.320
versus somewhere else.

244
00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.320
And you can easily go in here and magnify this

245
00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:50.610
by simply going in, go ahead and do another.

246
00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:54.610
I'm hitting Alt, and I'm moving this back here

247
00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:58.610
on the X-axis, and now you have several layers of fog,

248
00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:00.610
and if I wanted to, I could make another material

249
00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:01.610
and slow that one down.

250
00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.610
So they're not kind of moving in unison.

251
00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:05.610
So you get little different layers of movement.

252
00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:06.610
I'm not going to bother doing that,

253
00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:08.610
so that's pretty straightforward,

254
00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:10.610
but you can see how you can change that.

255
00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:12.610
The other material that I have in here,

256
00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:14.610
and we'll look at another fog card,

257
00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:16.610
is I'm going to pull back a bit,

258
00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:18.610
and you'll see that I have another one in here

259
00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:19.610
of a different color.

260
00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:21.610
This one actually works pretty well.

261
00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.610
This is used in one of our other scenes,

262
00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:26.610
but I'm making it accessible for you to play with.

263
00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:31.370
And let's go and grab them on the other side

264
00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:32.370
so it wants to grab the cube.

265
00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:33.370
There you go.

266
00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:37.970
So I have several different fog cards in here.

267
00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:41.570
Not just one, but two, and you can see them

268
00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:43.570
as I move my camera around.

269
00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:48.850
So these fog cards, excuse me, work really great.

270
00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.850
I can go in here with this particular fog card,

271
00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:54.139
and let's go and take a look at its blueprint.

272
00:16:54.139 --> 00:16:57.139
So it's trapped in a blueprint.

273
00:16:57.139 --> 00:16:59.139
We're going to go to our viewport.

274
00:16:59.139 --> 00:17:00.139
I'm going to click on that fog card,

275
00:17:00.139 --> 00:17:02.139
and his resolution is pretty cheap,

276
00:17:02.139 --> 00:17:04.140
but he's a bit more complex in his setup.

277
00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:06.299
So let's go to the parent material.

278
00:17:06.299 --> 00:17:10.130
In the parent material, if I click on the grid here,

279
00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:13.130
you'll see that it's a surface, an additive.

280
00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:18.599
So you want to make sure that in these particular fog cards,

281
00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:19.599
when you're making them,

282
00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:23.890
you're going to be using a translucent object.

283
00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:26.299
And you want to make sure,

284
00:17:26.299 --> 00:17:28.460
let's go back into that other one I had.

285
00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:30.660
You'll notice this one's translucent,

286
00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:33.660
so he's a bit different with his blend mode.

287
00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:37.230
But this other one I'm showing you is additive.

288
00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:38.710
Just want to point that out

289
00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:40.710
because it could be maybe a little bit confusing

290
00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:41.710
for some people.

291
00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:44.289
But you kind of get the same result.

292
00:17:44.289 --> 00:17:46.289
This just depends how you lay it out.

293
00:17:46.289 --> 00:17:49.289
Let's go back into that edit of the blueprint.

294
00:17:49.289 --> 00:17:50.289
Go to the viewport.

295
00:17:50.289 --> 00:17:52.349
Let's go back to that material.

296
00:17:52.349 --> 00:17:55.410
We'll go to the parent material.

297
00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:57.410
And you'll see this one is laid out

298
00:17:57.410 --> 00:17:59.410
a little bit more complex.

299
00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:00.410
There's a texture coordinate.

300
00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:03.410
There's the panning speed with a multiply and a time.

301
00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:05.410
And each one is going into a pattern.

302
00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.410
And it's nice that we make it

303
00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:09.410
look like it's three-dimensional.

304
00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:12.410
We have a tiling mask A and a tiling mask B.

305
00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:15.410
Each one going into a multiply,

306
00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:16.410
into a multiply here,

307
00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:19.410
and up above we have color we're pulling through.

308
00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:23.880
You'll notice we have a camera vector we're playing with

309
00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.880
and a pixel normal WS.

310
00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:29.880
And this allows us to mess with a transform vector here.

311
00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:32.900
And you'll see down below,

312
00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:34.900
oh, you'll notice we have an ABS.

313
00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:36.900
And now anytime you're not sure about these,

314
00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:38.900
feel free to do control-alt.

315
00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:39.900
It gives you a breakdown.

316
00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:40.970
This is an abbreviation

317
00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.970
for the mathematical term absolute value.

318
00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:46.970
So this gets our absolute value from this particular item

319
00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:48.970
taken in from that dot product,

320
00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:51.420
and allows us to be able to get

321
00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.700
some really nice camera vector movement and positioning.

322
00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.700
You'll notice down here it gets a little bit confusing,

323
00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.700
but it's really just several masks

324
00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:03.769
going in here with a minus and a lerp.

325
00:19:03.769 --> 00:19:05.769
Now, if you do run into problems,

326
00:19:05.769 --> 00:19:08.769
feel free to reorganize these any way that you want.

327
00:19:08.769 --> 00:19:12.180
It can be a noodle fest if you're not careful.

328
00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:13.180
But I gave this to you

329
00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:14.180
so you can actually play with it.

330
00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:16.180
This comes from one of our scenes.

331
00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:18.250
And it's a pretty simple, believe it or not,

332
00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:20.250
not complicated texture.

333
00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:22.250
If I go to my texture sample here,

334
00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:25.250
you'll see it's sand and dust storm mask.

335
00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:28.250
And again, we're using that depth fade in here

336
00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:31.250
with a soft edge all around scale

337
00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:34.309
that we're using within our alpha control in our lerp.

338
00:19:34.309 --> 00:19:35.309
Pretty nice.

339
00:19:35.309 --> 00:19:37.309
And you'll notice, though, this is key

340
00:19:37.309 --> 00:19:38.309
and kind of unusual for some people.

341
00:19:38.309 --> 00:19:41.309
You'll see that there's a customized UV.

342
00:19:41.309 --> 00:19:43.309
Yes, there is customized UVs that you can put in here.

343
00:19:43.309 --> 00:19:47.309
And if you want it needed to shift the UVs just a bit,

344
00:19:47.309 --> 00:19:48.309
you can.

345
00:19:48.309 --> 00:19:50.309
And you can just type in custom.

346
00:19:50.309 --> 00:19:53.309
Let me make sure I hit grid here.

347
00:19:53.309 --> 00:19:57.019
You can see a number of customized UVs.

348
00:19:57.019 --> 00:19:58.019
And you can put that in here

349
00:19:58.019 --> 00:20:01.430
and you can shift things a bit if you need it.

350
00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:03.430
That's kind of a rundown with that particular material.

351
00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:05.430
Let's go ahead and take a look at the volumetric material.

352
00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:09.430
And then we're going to unpack our fog in general

353
00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:10.430
and talk about some of the fog layers

354
00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:13.430
that you can create with your exponential height fog.

355
00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:15.430
So I'm clicking on this particular material,

356
00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:17.430
and we're not going to build the whole thing,

357
00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:19.430
but I'm just going to walk you through it.

358
00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:21.430
This particular material actually works pretty well.

359
00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:24.430
And again, these materials can be complex.

360
00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:28.460
But for some quick layers of fog and control,

361
00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:29.460
they can be priceless.

362
00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:33.460
You'll notice I have a distance amount, a fog magnifier.

363
00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:37.460
You'll see I also have a mass size, a mass size divide.

364
00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:40.779
If we go to the parent, you'll see them unpacked.

365
00:20:40.779 --> 00:20:42.779
And I have some notes in here too

366
00:20:42.779 --> 00:20:46.000
that help out a little bit.

367
00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.000
You'll see we have an absolute world position.

368
00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:52.000
And in here I have a mask size and mass size divide.

369
00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:56.000
Now what that does, if I want to adjust the look and feel

370
00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:01.670
between these coming in here, I can.

371
00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:04.670
So you'll see we have a mask input and a multiply.

372
00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:09.180
We have a panner and in here I added in the pen.

373
00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:11.180
Now we could go straight to a speed, overall speed,

374
00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:14.180
but I wanted to be able to control my U speed

375
00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:17.180
and my V speed and be able to separate those

376
00:21:17.180 --> 00:21:19.180
and not just have one speed control.

377
00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:23.180
So if I wanted to raise higher or go more vertical,

378
00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:24.180
I could do that.

379
00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:27.339
There's a mask for effect going into the texture

380
00:21:27.339 --> 00:21:29.339
and I'm using a thing called the three point levels.

381
00:21:29.339 --> 00:21:32.339
You'll see probably several tutorials online

382
00:21:32.339 --> 00:21:34.339
that talk about how to use this

383
00:21:34.339 --> 00:21:36.339
and they use it quite often.

384
00:21:36.339 --> 00:21:37.339
There's a mask edits.

385
00:21:37.339 --> 00:21:42.599
Mask edits, it's really just my four constant vector

386
00:21:42.599 --> 00:21:45.599
and I renamed them to go into my black value,

387
00:21:45.599 --> 00:21:47.599
middle value and white value.

388
00:21:47.599 --> 00:21:48.599
So you can mess with this color

389
00:21:48.599 --> 00:21:51.950
and relate to that to your object.

390
00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:53.950
You'll notice there's a static fog control

391
00:21:53.950 --> 00:21:54.950
that I created here too.

392
00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:57.950
Absolute world position, transform position

393
00:21:57.950 --> 00:21:59.950
and local object bounds.

394
00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:02.950
Now there are multiple ways again to do this and tackle it.

395
00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:05.950
But this gives you an idea of what you can do

396
00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:07.950
and you'll see our multiply comes to the very end here

397
00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:10.950
using an opacity to control extinction

398
00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:12.950
and we have the overall color.

399
00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:17.019
Now our color is also being influenced by our fog.

400
00:22:17.019 --> 00:22:19.019
If we go back to that material just for a second,

401
00:22:19.019 --> 00:22:21.019
you'll notice when I click on here,

402
00:22:21.019 --> 00:22:23.019
it is a volume material.

403
00:22:23.019 --> 00:22:27.079
So that volume material does require that fog be enabled.

404
00:22:27.079 --> 00:22:29.079
If you don't have fog enabled,

405
00:22:29.079 --> 00:22:31.079
this volume material is not gonna show up.

406
00:22:31.079 --> 00:22:32.079
So it's very complimentary.

407
00:22:32.079 --> 00:22:34.079
The fog cards are a bit different

408
00:22:34.079 --> 00:22:36.079
and what I mean by that if I type in fog

409
00:22:36.079 --> 00:22:41.980
and I turn off my exponential height fog,

410
00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:44.170
you'll notice that goes away

411
00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:47.740
but my fog cards are still present.

412
00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:49.740
So they're not dependent on it

413
00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:52.809
but that particular material is.

414
00:22:52.809 --> 00:22:55.190
Just keep those things in mind.

415
00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:59.700
So let's talk briefly about our fog layers.

416
00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:00.700
So if we go into our fog layers,

417
00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:04.700
you'll notice it's a lot foggier down there than up here.

418
00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:05.700
This is kind of status quo

419
00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:07.700
but down here it's a bit thicker.

420
00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:10.700
This is what I meant by an exponential height fog.

421
00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:13.700
You have a thing called secondary fog data.

422
00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:15.150
This is really great.

423
00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:18.150
You can control some extra fog data.

424
00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:21.150
Notice my scene really isn't being impacted

425
00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:25.150
because my height is down below versus up above.

426
00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:27.150
This can make it so that you have a scene

427
00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:30.150
where actors are up above looking down at a cliffside

428
00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:34.150
or an environment down below and one might have more fog.

429
00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.150
There's a lot of fun can be had here.

430
00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:39.539
You can control the fog height fall off

431
00:23:39.539 --> 00:23:41.539
and fog height offset.

432
00:23:41.539 --> 00:23:44.180
Also within your directional light,

433
00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:48.180
if you wanted to and your camera's facing the sun,

434
00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:52.180
you can use a thing called our god rays

435
00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:55.180
and you'll see that it's underneath light shafts.

436
00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:57.529
I have turned on light shaft occlusion

437
00:23:57.529 --> 00:23:58.529
in this particular case

438
00:23:58.529 --> 00:24:00.529
and we can see the difference between them.

439
00:24:00.529 --> 00:24:02.390
If I turn it off,

440
00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:05.390
we're straight using fog information

441
00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:07.390
and this is actually pretty realistic

442
00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:09.390
in the fact that if I turn my camera,

443
00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:14.680
those beams and shafts of fog light still stay present.

444
00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:17.680
The only downside of using this light shafts

445
00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:19.680
is it's based on your camera direction.

446
00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:21.680
So as soon as I turn my camera,

447
00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:26.059
those things will fade away and clip away.

448
00:24:26.059 --> 00:24:28.059
So it's not quite realistic.

449
00:24:28.059 --> 00:24:31.059
It's more of a screen space type of effect.

450
00:24:31.059 --> 00:24:33.059
There's even a light shaft bloom

451
00:24:33.059 --> 00:24:35.059
if you wanted to add more of that

452
00:24:35.059 --> 00:24:37.059
and you'll notice when I turn my camera,

453
00:24:37.059 --> 00:24:38.059
that really shows up,

454
00:24:38.059 --> 00:24:40.059
that screen space type of effect

455
00:24:40.059 --> 00:24:42.059
where it kind of clips away.

456
00:24:42.059 --> 00:24:44.059
So if your camera's facing, it's great.

457
00:24:44.059 --> 00:24:48.059
If it's not, they may not be exactly what you want.

458
00:24:48.059 --> 00:24:50.059
Let's keep those things in mind.

459
00:24:50.059 --> 00:24:53.150
All right, well that's about it

460
00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:56.150
with a rundown for fog when it comes to ICV effects.

461
00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:58.150
I hope that helped you out

462
00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:00.150
and you enjoyed this section of the course.
